---
import type { GetStaticPaths } from 'astro';
import EntryList from '../../components/EntryList.astro';
import Sidebar from '../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
	getPublishedPosts,
	postsBySolution,
	shouldShowSidebar,
	solutionFacets,
} from '../../lib/collections';

// Only solutions with at least one published entry get a page, so neither the
// rail, the search filter nor a row's eyebrow can link to an empty listing. An
// entry names several solutions, so it can appear under more than one of these.
//
// Filtered listings are not paginated: revisit if any single solution outgrows a
// comfortable page.
export const getStaticPaths = (async () => {
	const posts = await getPublishedPosts();

	return solutionFacets(posts).map((facet) => ({
		params: { solution: facet.slug },
		props: {
			label: facet.label,
			posts: postsBySolution(posts, facet.slug),
			allPosts: posts,
		},
	}));
}) satisfies GetStaticPaths;

const { label, posts, allPosts } = Astro.props;
const showSidebar = shouldShowSidebar(allPosts.length);
---

{
	/* The rail is here now, and it was deliberately absent before. Worth recording
	   why it changed, because the old reasoning was sound when it was written.

	   It said: the rail's only facet list is the products, and this page is one
	   product, so carrying the list here would put the current page in a list of
	   options - a filter offering itself as an option. It went on to say the way
	   sideways to another product is the header menu, which is on every page.

	   That second half stopped being true. The header menu listed the products
	   once; it has since become the five areas, so there is now no route from one
	   product archive to another anywhere on the site - which for a page whose
	   whole job is "here is what I have done with this product" is the one link a
	   reader is most likely to want next.

	   The original objection is answered rather than ignored: the rail renders the
	   current solution as plain text with aria-current, not as a link to the page
	   you are on. That is the same thing Pagination does with the current page and
	   Breadcrumb does with the last crumb.

	   The cost is that this route loads the whole collection again to render that
	   list, which it had stopped doing. Same shape as /topics/<slug> and
	   /archive/<year>, both of which take allPosts for the same reason.

	   Two crumbs, not three: there is no solutions index page to name as the middle
	   step - the rail is the list of them - and a crumb pointing at a page that does
	   not exist is worse than a shorter trail. */
}
<BaseLayout
	title={label}
	description={`Entries on ${label}.`}
	crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label }]}
>
	{
		/* No blurb under the heading. There is no written description of any of these
		   products anywhere in the repo, and inventing one here would be marketing
		   copy about Microsoft's product rather than anything about the work - which
		   the entries below already speak for. The count in the list header is the
		   one fact about the product this page can honestly add. */
	}
	<p class="text-text-3 text-[0.68rem] tracking-[0.12em] uppercase">Solution</p>
	<h1 class="font-display text-text mt-1 mb-6 text-2xl font-bold">{label}</h1>

	<div class={showSidebar ? 'grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-[1fr_15rem]' : ''}>
		<div class="min-w-0">
			{
				/* filteredBy solution: every row here shares the product in the heading
				   above, so the eyebrow that normally leads a row would repeat it down the
				   page and separate nothing. Those rows run title-first instead, with the
				   area and topics beneath - which is what actually distinguishes one from
				   the next here. */
			}
			<EntryList posts={posts} label="Entries" filteredBy={{ kind: 'solution' }} />
		</div>
		{showSidebar && <Sidebar posts={allPosts} currentSolution={label} />}
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
